The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = btrfs_next_leaf(root, path);
if (ret)
    break;
```
Reason:
The function `replay_dir_deletes` does not handle the error case properly when `btrfs_next_leaf()` returns a value indicating an error. If `ret` is non-zero, the function breaks out of the loop without properly signaling the error to the caller, which can lead to the return value `@ret` being incorrectly set to 0, misleading the caller into thinking the operation was successful.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret == 1)
    break;
else if (ret < 0)
    goto out;
```
Reason:
The patch modifies the error handling for `btrfs_next_leaf()`. It checks if `ret` is equal to 1 to break the loop, indicating a normal condition, while it checks if `ret` is less than 0 to handle the error case by jumping to the `out` label. This ensures that any errors encountered during the leaf traversal are properly propagated back to the caller, preventing the misleading return value.

